WEBVTT

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:05.560
<i>In the year SE 796,</i>

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.300
<i>also known to history as IC 487,</i>

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:12.500
<i>a Galactic Imperial Navy fleet of 
20,000 warships, led by Reinhard,</i>

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:17.200
<i>faced the Free Planets Alliance 
Navy in the Astarte Starzone.</i>

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.520
<i>The Alliance, with its superior numbers,</i>

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.710
<i>attempted to surround the 
Imperial Navy on three sides,</i>

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.260
<i>but the Imperials struck 
each of their fleets individually</i>

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:30.030
<i>led to the loss of the 4th and 6th 
Fleets, sealing the Alliance's defeat.</i>

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:35.210
<i>But then a voice heard in a message 
transmitted by the Alliance said,</i>

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:40.980
<i>"Do not worry. Obey my orders, 
and you will be saved."</i>

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.230
X Hours Earlier

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:44.260
<b>Legend of the Galactic Heroes</b>

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:52.230
<u>Free Planets Alliance Navy 2nd Fleet Flagship
</u>Patroklos

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:58.350
Yang Wen-li, reporting as ordered, sir.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:59.450
<u>2nd Fleet Deputy Staff Officer
</u>Commodore Yang Wen-li

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.540
I looked at the battle plan you submitted.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:06.060
It was a very interesting proposal.

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:09.210
<u>2nd Fleet Commander
</u>Vice Admiral Paetta

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.500
But I fear it may be overly prudent, 
perhaps even a bit passive.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:11.990
Is it, sir?

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:15.440
It's certainly hard to imagine 
we'd lose if we used it,

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.070
but there's no point in 
simply "not losing."

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:19.500
We have to <i>win</i>.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:22.790
Our forces have surrounded 
the enemy on three sides,

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:25.340
and we have twice their numbers.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.380
With all these factors on our side,

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:30.640
why must we simply contrive to not lose?

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:34.520
But, sir, our encirclement 
is not yet complete.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:38.480
In any case, I'm rejecting this proposal.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.240
Just so we're clear, this is 
nothing personal against you.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:46.770
Understood, sir.

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:49.200
Commodore!

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:54.620
And... you are?

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:56.310
Lao, a staff officer.

00:03:55.330 --> 00:04:00.090
<u>2nd Fleet Staff Officer
</u>Lieutenant Commander Lao

00:03:56.970 --> 00:04:00.330
It's an honor to be on the same 
team as the hero of El Facil.

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:03.350
Please, don't call me a hero.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.090
So, what did you need from me?

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:08.460
From the look on your face,

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:12.640
I would assume Vice Admiral Paetta 
didn't accept the plan you proposed.

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:14.310
You assume correctly.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:17.850
Did I look that depressed?

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:26.080
Might I ask how Your Excellency 
feels about our current situation?

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.060
We do have a clear advantage,

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:34.030
but if I were our enemy, I wouldn't 
consider myself in dire peril.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:39.300
Commodore.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.270
I have the luck of the devil.

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:47.490
In the past, I was one of three survivors 
when my fleet was destroyed.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:50.000
I certainly wish I could 
share that luck with you.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.010
As someone who's been through that, 
I'm optimistic about this mission.

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:56.370
Please, don't feel discouraged.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:58.590
Thanks...

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:05.650
<b>Episode 2</b>

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:05.650
<b>The Battle of Astarte</b>

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:09.460
Patrol craft 371 to flagship <i>Patroklos</i>.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:12.720
The Imperial Navy fleet could not be 
found in the anticipated sector.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:13.610
Repeat...

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:17.070
The Imperial Navy fleet could not be 
found in the anticipated sector.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.290
What? That doesn't make sense.

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:22.000
It's inconceivable!

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.600
Urgent transmission to the 
4th Fleet commander!

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.530
No response. It appears the enemy 
has jammed the radio waves.

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:31.310
Unable to establish a 
link to the 4th Fleet.

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:31.230
It turned out just like you said.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.180
Not that I'm happy about that.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:41.570
<i>With our encirclement still incomplete,</i>

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:44.370
<i>taking out our fleets individually 
would be the logical strategy.</i>

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:48.950
Meanwhile
Free Planets Alliance Navy 4th Fleet

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:51.470
<i>So they're not making the same mistake 
they made in the Slaughter at Dagon.</i>

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:01.170
Commodore Yang!

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:03.120
Yes, sir?

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:06.790
How do you view our situation? 
Tell me what you think.

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:12.080
The enemy must be planning to take 
out our fleets individually.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:16.810
The obvious choice would be to target the 
4th Fleet first, as it's the smallest.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:23.330
They've exercised their right to choose 
which of our scattered forces to confront.

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:27.000
Do you think the 4th Fleet can hold out?

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:32.380
The side that has the greater numbers and 
makes the first move has the advantage.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:36.730
In any case, we need to rush to the 
battlefield and aid the 4th Fleet.

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:40.440
If we're lucky, it's possible we could 
strike the Imperial Navy's rear flank.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.340
That will give us an instant advantage.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:44.960
I don't believe that's possible.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:46.710
What do you mean?

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:51.100
The battle will have ended 
by the time we get there.

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:58.750
The enemy will leave to strike the rear 
flank of either the 2nd or the 6th Fleet.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:01.210
We've lost the initiative.

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:03.900
In fact, they've taken it 
from us at every turn.

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:07.870
It's my opinion that there is no need 
for us to play into the enemy's hands.

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:10.060
What do you suggest we do, then?!

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:11.820
Change our procedure.

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:15.330
Instead of departing to aid the 4th Fleet,

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:18.370
we merge with the 6th Fleet 
as soon as possible

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:21.340
and create a new battlefield 
in that sector.

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:24.840
The two fleets combined 
would total 28,000 ships,

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:28.600
so we would face the enemy with higher 
than fifty-fifty odds of victory.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:32.340
You're suggesting we leave 
the 4th Fleet to die?

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:35.760
If we left now, we still 
wouldn't reach them in time.

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:37.440
You can't be certain of that!

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:41.090
The 4th Fleet won't be so easily defeated.

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:43.270
If they can hold out...

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:47.350
I've already advised you 
that it's impossible.

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:52.580
Commodore Yang, reality doesn't 
rely solely on your calculations!

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:56.210
The one leading their expeditionary 
force is Count Lohengramm.

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:58.110
He is young and inexperienced.

00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:01.780
Commander, sir, you say 
he is inexperienced,

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:03.390
but his strategic ideas are—

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:04.760
Enough, Commodore!

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:07.680
Vice Admiral Pastolle is a veteran.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:09.510
He will not go down easily!

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:28.900
Jean, we were ordered to 
halt communications.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:31.520
<i>The enemy isn't in the area where 
we expected to find them.</i>

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:32.840
I know.

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:34.490
<i>No hope for the 4th Fleet, then?</i>

00:08:34.490 --> 00:08:35.870
Most likely not.

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:39.190
The enemy will probably go 
after the 6th Fleet next.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:43.320
<i>If so, they'll increase the interference 
on our communications.</i>

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:45.580
<i>That means this is the last time I contact you.</i>

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:48.200
<i>Let's both do everything we can.</i>

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:52.500
<i>I'll advise Vice Admiral Moore to 
rendezvous with the 2nd Fleet.</i>

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:56.130
I'll try advising Vice Admiral 
Paetta again, as well, but...

00:08:56.390 --> 00:08:59.840
<i>Yang, if anything happens to 
me, take care of Jessica.</i>

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:02.180
Don't start talking like that.

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:04.340
<i>And you call yourself a military man?</i>

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:07.140
It's why I don't really fit 
in with the military.

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:09.850
Stay alive and go home to Jessica.

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:12.900
I don't want to hear dying 
requests from you.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:14.560
<i>All right, Yang.</i>

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:16.590
<i>I wish you luck.</i>

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:20.490
Signal lost.

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:22.820
Don't die, Jean.

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:25.000
Free Planets Alliance Navy 6th Fleet

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.090
Enemy attack from the right rear flank!

00:09:25.090 --> 00:09:28.110
The enemy jam has severed our data link!

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:29.870
Shields incomplete!

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:38.300
Transmission from the Imperial Navy.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:40.750
"You are completely surrounded.

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:42.460
You have no escape.

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:45.780
Surrender. You will be shown mercy."

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:54.430
<i>Jessica...</i>

00:09:58.830 --> 00:09:59.810
Your Excellency.

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:02.570
You can see the situation we're in.

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:07.940
The enemy is certain to target 
our own 2nd Fleet next.

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:12.530
Commodore Yang, do you have any 
ideas that aren't passive?

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:16.690
I haven't given any advice 
that was not proactive.

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:20.020
Radio signal jammed. 
Scouting system partially offline.

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:22.840
Optical signal detecting approaching ship.

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:25.780
Analyzing image. 
Most likely an enemy craft.

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:28.640
Type I order to all crafts! 
Report to battle stations!

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:29.420
Now!

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:32.210
<i>That was faster than I expected.</i>

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:04.380
Your Excellency!

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:08.420
Commander Paetta is wounded.

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:10.560
Ready the sick bay immediately.

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:13.600
Are any officers still alive in here?

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:15.570
I'm all right.

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:16.780
Lieutenant Commander Lao.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:22.090
I told you... I have the luck of the devil.

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:28.780
Commodore Yang, take command of the fleet.

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:30.370
Me, sir?

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:33.990
It seems you are the highest ranked 
among the surviving officers.

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:38.290
We need your skills as a tactician...

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:41.970
I guess he really respected you, deep down.

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:43.290
You think so?

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:48.220
Message to all fleets.

00:11:48.490 --> 00:11:52.800
I am Commodore Yang Wen-li, 
deputy staff officer to Commander Paetta.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:55.470
Our flagship, <i>Patroklos</i>, has been hit,

00:11:55.470 --> 00:11:58.390
and Commander Paetta is gravely injured.

00:11:58.390 --> 00:12:02.590
Per the commander's orders, I will 
take over command of all fleets.

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:09.480
<i>Do not worry. Obey my orders, 
and you will be saved.</i>

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:13.740
<i>Those who wish to survive, 
remain calm and follow my directions.</i>

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:17.190
<i>Our force is losing the 
battle at the moment,</i>

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:20.430
<i>but all that matters is 
that we win in the end.</i>

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:22.360
<i>We will not lose.</i>

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:35.730
All ships, focus on destroying individual 
crafts until I give further orders.

00:12:45.260 --> 00:12:46.830
"We will not lose"...

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:50.240
"Obey my orders, and you 
will be saved," eh?

00:12:51.160 --> 00:12:55.930
I never knew the rebel forces had 
such a braggart among their ranks.

00:12:56.790 --> 00:13:01.290
How does he intend to turn his 
disadvantage around at this stage?

00:13:02.870 --> 00:13:04.150
Well, no matter.

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:06.840
Let's see what he's got, Kircheis.

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:07.690
Yes.

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:37.150
The enemy is reforming into 
a spindle formation.

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:39.800
They must be planning to 
break through our center.

00:13:40.850 --> 00:13:42.760
What will we do?

00:13:42.760 --> 00:13:44.460
I have a plan in mind.

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:47.310
But how will you give the order?

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:50.760
The enemy is still jamming 
our communications.

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:53.400
The data link isn't restored yet, either.

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:55.470
How can we...

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:57.070
Don't worry.

00:13:57.070 --> 00:14:00.070
Broadcast a compressed data transmission
across all frequency ranges.

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:04.810
Tell each ship to open code C-4 on their tactical intel system.

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:08.380
Even if the enemy's monitoring us, 
they can't figure anything out

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:10.190
if that's all we say.

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:16.880
<i>Even then, he already...</i>

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:20.960
I had hoped this wouldn't 
be necessary, but alas...

00:14:24.710 --> 00:14:27.170
<u>2nd Fleet Squadron Flagship
</u>Manannan Mac Lir

00:14:27.350 --> 00:14:32.350
Chief engineer, reroute all output 
except life support to the shields.

00:14:32.350 --> 00:14:34.650
Order from flagship <i>Patroklos</i>.

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:38.240
It says to enable Code C-4 on the 
tactical information system.

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:40.190
Do so at once.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:44.440
This is...

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:48.150
It's a strategic plan for countering 
a breakthrough to our center.

00:14:48.410 --> 00:14:52.270
Commodore Yang Wen-li 
prepared this in advance?

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:53.370
<u>2nd Fleet Squadron Commander
</u>Commodore Edwin Fisher

00:14:54.110 --> 00:14:57.230
In accordance with the 
Code C-4 strategic plan,

00:14:57.230 --> 00:14:59.300
we will henceforth move as a united fleet.

00:14:59.300 --> 00:15:01.320
Don't rely on the data link.

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:04.600
All ships, commence retreat independently.

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:05.830
Commencing retreat.

00:15:15.290 --> 00:15:16.930
It's working out so far.

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:20.520
The rest depends on whether our 
men act as we need them to.

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:24.430
Yes, one misstep and we'll lose all control.

00:15:25.220 --> 00:15:27.390
What will you do if that happens?

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:31.520
Pretend I was clueless.

00:15:40.610 --> 00:15:42.000
Is something the matter?

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:48.700
Reinhard?

00:15:48.700 --> 00:15:51.210
It's strange. Something isn't right.

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:54.860
We're winning. There's no doubt about that.

00:15:55.590 --> 00:15:56.460
But...

00:16:02.050 --> 00:16:03.410
No!

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:25.980
The enemy has split up and is advancing on 
us at high speed from both sides!

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:27.810
They got us.

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:30.770
They made it look like 
they'd been broken up,

00:16:30.770 --> 00:16:34.400
then split into two forces 
to come around behind us.

00:16:39.250 --> 00:16:42.800
Our strategy to break through 
their center backfired!

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:49.190
What?!

00:16:52.300 --> 00:16:55.600
What will you do? 
Reverse course and intercept them?

00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:59.470
Don't make jokes. 
Are you telling me to be incompetent?

00:16:59.470 --> 00:17:01.860
More incompetent than the 
enemy's 4th Fleet commander?

00:17:01.860 --> 00:17:04.720
In that case, our only option 
is to move forward.

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:06.080
Precisely.

00:17:07.370 --> 00:17:09.540
All fleets, full speed ahead!

00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:12.040
Come right up their tails 
as they turn back on us!

00:17:44.300 --> 00:17:46.680
I've never seen a formation 
like this before.

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:49.500
I'd imagine not. I haven't, either.

00:17:50.450 --> 00:17:53.270
<i>No, this isn't the first time I've seen it.</i>

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:58.590
<i>This has happened in battlefields 
everywhere since the dawn of time.</i>

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:01.470
What a stupid-looking formation!

00:18:01.470 --> 00:18:03.930
This is going to turn into 
a battle of attrition!

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:26.100
The enemy should begin to pull back soon.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:28.370
Then are we going to pursue them?

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:32.380
Let's not.

00:18:32.380 --> 00:18:35.570
We'll retreat when they do.

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:38.200
We're already doing all we're capable of.

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:42.740
Kircheis, what do you think?

00:18:42.740 --> 00:18:45.130
I think perhaps it's time to end this.

00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:49.330
Continuing this battle will only produce 
more casualties on both sides.

00:18:49.770 --> 00:18:52.040
There is no strategic purpose to this.

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:55.440
Does that frustrate you?

00:18:55.890 --> 00:18:59.480
Not especially, but I would 
have liked more of a win.

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:02.790
It's too bad we couldn't apply 
the finishing touches.

00:19:04.870 --> 00:19:07.800
Despite being surrounded by 
the enemy on three sides,

00:19:07.800 --> 00:19:11.180
you eliminated two of their fleets with 
a strategy to strike them individually.

00:19:11.180 --> 00:19:13.950
Then, with the last of the enemy reversing 
course to come up behind you,

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:15.820
you fought on even footing.

00:19:15.820 --> 00:19:17.670
Is that not good enough?

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:23.010
To hope for any more than that 
would be a bit too greedy.

00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:25.730
I know that.

00:19:38.140 --> 00:19:40.360
The commander of their 2nd Fleet...

00:19:42.020 --> 00:19:44.620
The man who assumed 
command during the battle...

00:19:44.620 --> 00:19:46.240
What was his name again?

00:19:46.240 --> 00:19:48.240
Commodore Yang Wen-li.

00:19:48.240 --> 00:19:50.240
Right, Yang.

00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:55.550
He just might be the kind of 
person you spoke of earlier.

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:04.640
Send a message to Commodore 
Yang Wen-li in my name.

00:20:05.150 --> 00:20:06.460
Certainly.

00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:10.010
Now I have something new 
to look forward to.

00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:27.900
Acting Commander Yang, there's a message 
for you from the Imperial Navy.

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:30.040
For me? Read it.

00:20:31.330 --> 00:20:33.740
"I offer my respect for 
your bravery in battle.

00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:35.760
I pray for your good health 
until we do battle again.

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:40.570
Galactic Imperial Navy High Admiral 
Reinhard von Lohengramm."

00:20:41.060 --> 00:20:42.020
That's all.

00:20:42.690 --> 00:20:45.480
Respect for my bravery in battle, eh?

00:20:45.480 --> 00:20:46.890
How humbling.

00:20:47.710 --> 00:20:50.140
What shall we do? Will you respond?

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:54.140
I doubt he expects that of me.

00:20:55.310 --> 00:20:57.480
No matter. Just leave it be.

00:20:57.900 --> 00:21:00.640
More importantly, we must quickly 
retrieve any survivors.

00:21:01.110 --> 00:21:03.410
I want to save as many as we can.

00:21:04.280 --> 00:21:05.410
Understood.

00:21:07.640 --> 00:21:11.330
Thanks to you, my life is spared yet again.

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:14.330
You just have good luck.

00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:32.310
<i>And thus the Battle of 
Astarte came to an end.</i>

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:38.280
<i>The Alliance suffered ten to eleven 
times the losses of the Imperial Navy,</i>

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:43.850
<i>but they barely managed to stop the Galactic 
Empire's invasion of the Astarte Starzone.</i>

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:28.590
<u>Galactic Imperial Palace
</u>Neue Sanssouci

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:47.360
The leader of all mankind, 
ruler of all the universe,

00:23:47.360 --> 00:23:50.760
keeper of all order and 
law under the heavens,

00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:53.750
the holy and inviolable kaiser 
of the Galactic Empire,

00:23:53.750 --> 00:23:56.530
His Highness Friedrich IV!

00:24:08.340 --> 00:24:12.680
<u>36th Kaiser of the Galactic Empire
</u>Friedrich IV

00:24:13.170 --> 00:24:16.150
Count Reinhard von Lohengramm.

00:24:25.410 --> 00:24:29.120
<i>On this day, the high admiral 
of the Galactic Imperial Navy,</i>

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:33.380
<i>Count Reinhard von Lohengramm, 
was named the Imperial Fleet Admiral.</i>

00:24:34.070 --> 00:24:40.790
<i>However, this was but one minor 
step toward his true destination.</i>

00:24:50.260 --> 00:25:00.270
<b>Episode 3</b>

00:24:50.260 --> 00:25:00.270
<b>An Indomitable Prodigy</b>

00:24:50.790 --> 00:24:54.960
<i>Next time on </i>Legend of 
 the Galactic Heroes - Die Neue These<i>,</i>

00:24:55.590 --> 00:24:59.370
<i>episode three, "An Indomitable Prodigy."</i>
